  "summary": "The great white sphere of Joseon porcelain — two hemispheres joined, imperfect, luminous.",
  "description": "The moon jar (dalhangari) throws two large hemispheres and joins them at the belly, so the finished sphere always leans, dimples or sags slightly — and Joseon taste loved exactly that: white slip-painted porcelain with faint iron freckles, glaze pooling blue-green at the waist seam, presence without ornament. Court jars held flowers and fruit; today the form is Korea's national ceramic icon, re-thrown by masters from Yu Geun-Hyeong's revival onward, and a favorite of contemporary ceramists worldwide.",
